;; status-present — a claim must have a status value (minCount 1)
;; (REQ-KG-012). P2.4 closure of the external-audit gap: the EDN->Cozo port
;; carried tbf:status's sh:in vocabulary (status-enum.edn) but NOT its
;; sh:minCount 1, so under KG_BACKEND=cozo a status-less claim CONFORMED while
;; pyshacl flagged it.
;;
;; Booklogic port of the tbf:status minCount constraint in assets/shapes.ttl:
;;   sh:path tbf:status ; sh:minCount 1 .
;;
;; minCount >= 1 lowers to a NEGATION exactly like text-cardinality: a claim is a
;; violation when NO claim row with that id carries a non-null status. Both :not
;; triples share the entity var ?c2 so they collapse to one negated group; ?focus
;; is bound by the positive :where while ?status is FREE (it appears only in the
;; :not). The compiler lifts the free-var negation into a named
;; ``status_present_<n>`` helper rule guarded by !is_null(status), then negates it
;; over ?focus — so a truly-absent status fires, an empty string (non-null)
;; conforms, matching SHACL minCount "a value EXISTS". A plain inline negation
;; would bind ?status to a NULL cell and wrongly exclude the absent-value row (see
;; scripts/booklogic_kg._compile_constraint_negations).
;;
;; :component is the SHACL sourceConstraintComponent this constraint ports. It is
;; NOT read by the compiler (the EDN head is the fixed violation tuple); it is the
;; disambiguator the rdflib message-remap keys on, because status-present and
;; status-enum share the tbf:status path but carry distinct authored messages
;; (audit I-2 — a path-only key would collapse them).
(defconstraint :status-present
  :message "Claim must have a status value (minCount 1)."
  :path "https://example.org/book-knowledge#status"
  :component "http://www.w3.org/ns/shacl#MinCountConstraintComponent"
  :where [[?c :claim/id ?focus]]
  :not   [[?c2 :claim/id ?focus]
          [?c2 :claim/status ?status]])
